\DOC LOCAL_ADD5_4_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_ADD5_4_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances a symbolic-execution goal across one inlined modular-addition
subroutine call, replacing the current state variable by a successor state and
adding assumptions describing the subroutine's result.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int}, an offset/index selecting the call site within
the surrounding code. The second is a {string list} naming the logical
variables for the operation's output and its two inputs: the first name becomes
the new result variable, while the other two name the already-known input
quantities whose bound and congruence appear in the new assumption. The goal's
conclusion is an {eventually} statement about a state variable; it becomes the
same statement about a fresh successor state variable. The assumptions about
the old state — the program counter value, {bytes_loaded}, register and
memory-content readings, and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} frame — are discharged
and re-added phrased in the new state, with the program counter advanced and
the {MAYCHANGE} frame extended by the newly written memory region; the input
assumption for the consumed operand is dropped. Two new assumptions appear: an
equation reading the output memory region of the new state as the freshly named
result variable, and an implication bounding that variable and giving its
congruence to the sum of the two named inputs. Exactly one subgoal results.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces, only this single kind of transformation occurs, always
on goals carrying a large assumption list of this symbolic-execution form.

\ENDDOC
